{{define "docs-deploy"}}
<div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-10 flex gap-12">
    {{template "sidebar" .}}

    <article class="prose flex-1 max-w-3xl">
        <h1>Deploy</h1>
        <p class="lede">One static binary plus a <code>dist/</code> directory.
        That's it: no node runtime in production, no separate frontend service.</p>

        <h2 id="build">task build</h2>
        <p>Two steps, run for you by the bundled Taskfile:</p>
        <pre class="code-block" data-lang="bash"><code>$ task build
# 1. npm run build    → dist/assets/{main.js, style.css, ...}
# 2. go build -ldflags='-s -w' -o bin/app .
# Built bin/app (12M)</code></pre>
        <p>The Go binary doesn't read <code>node_modules/</code> at runtime; everything
        the browser needs is in <code>dist/assets/</code>. <code>-s -w</code> strips the
        debug table so the binary is meaningfully smaller (~30% in our experience).</p>

        <h2 id="serve">task serve</h2>
        <pre class="code-block" data-lang="bash"><code>$ task serve
# sets PRODUCTION=true and runs ./bin/app</code></pre>
        <p>The <code>PRODUCTION=true</code> env var flips the
        <code>{{`{{if .development}}`}}</code> branch in <code>base.tmpl</code> from "load
        from Vite dev server" to "load from <code>/dist/assets/</code>". So the same
        templates work in both modes.</p>

        <h2 id="env-vars">Environment variables</h2>
        <table class="text-sm border-collapse w-full my-4">
            <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">
                    <th class="text-left font-medium py-2 pr-4">Variable</th>
                    <th class="text-left font-medium py-2 pr-4">Default</th>
                    <th class="text-left font-medium py-2">Purpose</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
                <tr class="border-b border-slate-100 dark:border-slate-900">
                    <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">PORT</td>
                    <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">8080</td>
                    <td class="py-2">HTTP listen port</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-slate-100 dark:border-slate-900">
                    <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">PRODUCTION</td>
                    <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">false</td>
                    <td class="py-2">Switches templates to <code>/dist/</code> assets</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">VITE_DEV_SERVER</td>
                    <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">http://localhost:3000</td>
                    <td class="py-2">Where dev mode loads JS/CSS from</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h2 id="output">What's in dist/</h2>
        <pre class="code-block" data-lang="bash"><code>dist/
└── assets/
    ├── main.js        # Vite bundle of src/main.js (htmx + your code)
    └── style.css      # Tailwind output (only classes you actually used)</code></pre>
        <p>The vendored runtime files in <code>static/js/</code> (<code>hyperchi-directives.js</code>
        and <code>hyperchi-view-transitions.js</code>) are served as-is by the Go server.
        They don't go through Vite.</p>

        <h2 id="docker">Docker</h2>
        <p>Two-stage build with a static binary:</p>
        <details class="code-gate">
            <summary>Show Dockerfile</summary>
            <pre class="code-block" data-lang="dockerfile"><code># --- frontend stage ---
FROM node:20-alpine AS frontend
WORKDIR /app
COPY package.json package-lock.json ./
RUN npm ci
COPY . .
RUN npm run build

# --- go stage ---
FROM golang:1.26-alpine AS backend
WORKDIR /app
COPY go.mod go.sum ./
RUN go mod download
COPY . .
COPY --from=frontend /app/dist ./dist
RUN CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -ldflags='-s -w' -o /out/app .

# --- runtime ---
FROM gcr.io/distroless/static
COPY --from=backend /out/app /app
COPY --from=backend /app/dist /dist
COPY --from=backend /app/static /static
COPY --from=backend /app/templates /templates
ENV PRODUCTION=true PORT=8080
EXPOSE 8080
USER nonroot
ENTRYPOINT ["/app"]</code></pre>
        </details>

        <h2 id="hosts">Hosting shapes</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>VM (Hetzner, EC2, droplet)</strong>: copy the binary, the
            <code>dist/</code>, <code>static/</code>, <code>templates/</code> dirs;
            run under systemd. Smallest moving parts.</li>
            <li><strong>Fly.io / Railway / Render</strong>: a Dockerfile like the one
            above plus a port env var. Both honor <code>PORT</code> automatically.</li>
            <li><strong>Behind a CDN</strong>: point the CDN at the Go server; cache
            <code>/dist/*</code> and <code>/static/*</code> aggressively, leave HTML
            uncached. The Go process becomes the origin.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="security">Production checklist</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Set a strong CSP. The default in <code>middleware.Security()</code>
            permits <code>'unsafe-eval'</code> because the directive engine and
            <code>hx-on</code> attributes use <code>new Function()</code>.</li>
            <li>Keep CSRF protection on. <code>app.AutoMiddleware()</code> installs it,
            and the layout sends <code>.csrfToken</code> with every htmx request.</li>
            <li>Run tests in CI: <code>go test ./...</code></li>
            <li>Set <code>ENVIRONMENT=production</code> at the supervisor level, not in code,
            and a <code>SECRET_KEY</code> of 32 bytes or more if the app signs cookies or uses flash messages.</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="text-sm pt-6">
            <a href="/" hx-boost="true">← Back to start</a>
            &nbsp;·&nbsp;
            <a href="/docs/directives" hx-boost="true">Re-read directives</a>
        </p>
    </article>
</div>
{{end}}
